      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The decision about whether one wishes to carry out a Bayesian analysis should depend on whether",
      "bbox": [
        57.055999755859375,
        390.3300476074219,
        443.6065368652344,
        398.64190673828125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "one wishes to impose a prior distribution on unknown parameters. Here, I have shown that likelihood-",
      "bbox": [
        48.089599609375,
        400.2926025390625,
        443.6063537597656,
        408.6044616699219
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "based non-Bayesian methodology provides a computationally viable alternative to the authors’ Bayesian",
      "bbox": [
        48.089599609375,
        410.2551574707031,
        443.6083984375,
        418.5670166015625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "approach for complex dynamic models.",
      "bbox": [
        48.089599609375,
        420.21771240234375,
        196.94081115722656,
        428.5295715332031
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Luke Bornn and Aline Tabet (University of British Columbia, Vancouver)",
      "bbox": [
        48.089599609375,
        440.1428527832031,
        319.2918395996094,
        448.4547119140625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "We congratulate the authors on this very important contribution to stochastic computation in statistics.",
      "bbox": [
        48.089630126953125,
        450.1054382324219,
        443.61187744140625,
        458.41729736328125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Whereas the authors have explored and discussed several applications in the paper, we would like to high-",
      "bbox": [
        48.089630126953125,
        460.0679931640625,
        443.6119689941406,
        468.3798522949219
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "light the beneﬁts of using particle Markov chain Monte Carlo (PMCMC) methods as a way to extend",
      "bbox": [
        48.089630126953125,
        470.0305480957031,
        443.6126708984375,
        478.3424072265625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "sequential Monte Carlo (SMC) methods which employ sequences of distributions of static dimension.",
      "bbox": [
        48.089630126953125,
        479.9931335449219,
        443.6092529296875,
        488.30499267578125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Through PMCMC sampling, we can separate the variables of interest into those which may be easily sam-",
      "bbox": [
        48.089630126953125,
        489.9557189941406,
        443.6091003417969,
        498.267578125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "pled by using traditional MCMC techniques and those which require a more specialized SMC approach.",
      "bbox": [
        48.089630126953125,
        499.91827392578125,
        443.60455322265625,
        508.2301330566406
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Consider for instance the use of simulated annealing in an SMC framework (Neal, 2001; Del Moral",
      "bbox": [
        48.089630126953125,
        509.8808288574219,
        443.6056823730469,
        518.1926879882812
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "et al., 2006). Rather than ﬁnding the posterior maximum a posteriori estimate of all parameters, PMCMC",
      "bbox": [
        48.089630126953125,
        519.8434448242188,
        443.6028747558594,
        528.1552734375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "sampling now allows practitioners to combine annealing with traditional MCMC methods to maximize",
      "bbox": [
        48.0887451171875,
        529.8060302734375,
        443.6100158691406,
        538.1178588867188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "over some dimensions simultaneously while exploring the full posterior in others.",
      "bbox": [
        48.0887451171875,
        539.7685546875,
        354.42584228515625,
        548.0803833007812
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "When variables are highly correlated, SMC methods may be used as an efﬁcient alternative to MCMC",
      "bbox": [
        57.055145263671875,
        549.7311401367188,
        443.6100158691406,
        558.04296875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "sampling. For instance, SMC samplers (Del Moral et al., 2006) and other population-based methods (Jasra",
      "bbox": [
        48.0887451171875,
        559.6937255859375,
        443.60479736328125,
        568.0055541992188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "et al., 2007) proceed by working through a sequence of auxiliary distributions until a particle-based approx-",
      "bbox": [
        48.08876037597656,
        569.6563110351562,
        443.6092224121094,
        577.9681396484375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "imation to the posterior is reached. In non-identiﬁable or weakly identiﬁable models, SMC sampling is used",
      "bbox": [
        48.08876037597656,
        579.618896484375,
        443.604736328125,
        587.9307250976562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "to construct a sequence of tempered distributions allowing particles to explore fully the resulting ridges",
      "bbox": [
        48.08876037597656,
        589.5814208984375,
        443.60919189453125,
        597.8932495117188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "in the posterior surface of the non-identiﬁable variables. However, because SMC algorithms often rely on",
      "bbox": [
        48.08876037597656,
        599.5440063476562,
        443.6091003417969,
        607.8558349609375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "importance sampling, they can suffer in high dimensions owing to increased variability in the importance",
      "bbox": [
        48.08876037597656,
        609.506591796875,
        443.61102294921875,
        617.8184204101562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "weights. Many non-identiﬁable models contain only a small portion of variables with identiﬁability issues,",
      "bbox": [
        48.08876037597656,
        619.4691162109375,
        443.6111145019531,
        627.7809448242188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    }
